WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:07.924
FAYE MOSS: Hello, and welcome to Superstars in History . I'm your host,

2
00:00:07.925 --> 00:00:14.522
Faye Moss. Today's guest knows better than most people that it's not easy being

3
00:00:14.523 --> 00:00:24.707
a queen! Her reign is viewed now as a golden age, but her path to the crown was surprising—and sometimes even dangerous.

4
00:00:24.708 --> 00:00:47.721
Please give a royal welcome to Her Majesty Queen Elizabeth I of England and Ireland!

5
00:00:47.722 --> 00:00:51.295
QUEEN ELIZABETH I: Thank you, child, for inviting me to

6
00:00:51.296 --> 00:00:57.907
your court. FAYE MOSS: Of course, Your Majesty. You

7
00:00:57.908 --> 00:01:03.314
were born in 1533. Through most of your childhood, it seemed unlikely that you

8
00:01:03.315 --> 00:01:10.130
would become queen. And then you did! Please tell us how you

9
00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:16.363
got there. QUEEN ELIZABETH I: I was the first child of King Henry VIII

10
00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:27.175
of England and Anne Boleyn. As their daughter, I was a princess and heir to the throne, but no one thought I would ever become queen—England had never crowned

11
00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:33.852
a queen before! In fact, my father had divorced his first wife because she hadn't given birth to

12
00:01:33.853 --> 00:01:38.604
a son. Their only child was a daughter, my half

13
00:01:38.605 --> 00:01:43.336
sister, Mary. FAYE MOSS: It sounds like your father really

14
00:01:43.337 --> 00:01:49.845
wanted a son! QUEEN ELIZABETH I: Yes, my father was desperate to have a male heir who

15
00:01:49.846 --> 00:01:54.316
would succeed him one day. Alas, he was married to my mother

16
00:01:54.317 --> 00:02:02.038
for only a few years. I was just two when she became the victim of a political plot and

17
00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.783
was jailed

18
00:02:04.784 --> 00:02:12.321
and beheaded. FAYE MOSS: That's awful! QUEEN ELIZABETH I: Indeed it was, Faye. However,

19
00:02:12.322 --> 00:02:17.507
my father loved me and treated me well. I was given

20
00:02:17.508 --> 00:02:23.190
an excellent education and learned several languages. I received the kind of education a male heir

21
00:02:23.191 --> 00:02:32.068
to the throne would have had. A male heir was finally born to my father and his third wife, Jane Seymour, who died shortly

22
00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:39.082
after Prince Edward was born. My father married three more times in the hope of having more sons, but it was

23
00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:46.917
not meant to be. When he died in 1547, nine-year-old Edward

24
00:02:46.918 --> 00:02:56.077
became King Edward VI. After less than seven years on the throne, Edward fell ill and died. Mary

25
00:02:56.078 --> 00:03:03.362
then became queen. FAYE MOSS: That made you next in line to the throne. What were those

26
00:03:03.363 --> 00:03:12.021
years like for you? QUEEN ELIZABETH I: They were dangerous! To divorce his first wife, my father had separated

27
00:03:12.022 --> 00:03:18.781
England from the Roman Catholic Church. This created a lot of religious tension at

28
00:03:18.782 --> 00:03:25.349
court and in the country. Mary had remained a Catholic and returned England to the Catholic Church

29
00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:32.024
when she became queen. In addition she married Prince Philip of Spain, also a Catholic, and hoped to

30
00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:39.657
have a son. Since I had a claim to the throne but wasn't thought to be a Catholic, Mary was suspicious

31
00:03:39.658 --> 00:03:45.580
of me. After a rebellion, in which I played no part, I was kept under

32
00:03:45.581 --> 00:03:50.842
arrest. Eventually, when she thought she was pregnant and would have an heir, I was

33
00:03:50.843 --> 00:03:54.448
released. However, her child never came.

34
00:03:54.449 --> 00:03:58.174
FAYE MOSS: Those must have been difficult years

35
00:03:58.175 --> 00:04:05.876
for you. QUEEN ELIZABETH I: I privately opposed my sister's religious policy but had to be careful to always show

36
00:04:05.877 --> 00:04:11.781
my loyalty to her. I developed a lot of diplomatic skill during these years that served

37
00:04:11.782 --> 00:04:15.746
me well later. I learned that it is often best to

38
00:04:15.747 --> 00:04:21.933
keep silent. And when one does speak, it's prudent not to say everything that one

39
00:04:21.934 --> 00:04:31.253
is thinking. In the fourth year of her reign, Mary thought she was pregnant again, but no child came, and it was clear that she was in poor health.

40
00:04:31.254 --> 00:04:39.872
In 1558 she died, and I became Queen of England and Ireland. I was 25 years old. FAYE

41
00:04:39.873 --> 00:04:43.831
MOSS: Unlike Queen Mary, you never

42
00:04:43.832 --> 00:04:50.109
married. QUEEN ELIZABETH I: Marriage and having children was important, but it came with risks, especially

43
00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:56.319
for a woman. I would have had to share my power with my husband, to whom I was expected to

44
00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.304
be obedient. My sister's marriage to a foreign royal had

45
00:05:01.305 --> 00:05:06.652
been unpopular. But choosing one Englishman could have created resentment among them

46
00:05:06.653 --> 00:05:11.374
all. Rather than marry a man, I was married to my country.

47
00:05:11.375 --> 00:05:17.798
As you can see, I wear my coronation ring on my wedding finger.

48
00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:22.934
FAYE MOSS: I guess your decision helped keep England stable during your

49
00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:28.590
reign. QUEEN ELIZABETH I: Ensuring stability was the most important task I

50
00:05:28.591 --> 00:05:34.420
faced as queen. For many years England had suffered a series of power struggles

51
00:05:34.421 --> 00:05:41.596
and civil wars. I was able to bring a moderation to the country that kept factions from tearing us

52
00:05:41.597 --> 00:05:50.761
in two. I firmly established the Church of England, but unlike Mary, I made changes gradually, and with the love and support of the people.

53
00:05:50.762 --> 00:05:58.024
Not only did I seek to prevent wars at home, but it was also my aim to avoid foreign wars. FAYE

54
00:05:58.025 --> 00:06:02.105
MOSS: Yes, but you had a close call with

55
00:06:02.106 --> 00:06:13.294
Spain. QUEEN ELIZABETH I: In 1588 Spain sent a great fleet of warships, the Spanish Armada, to overthrow me and return England to

56
00:06:13.295 --> 00:06:22.589
the Catholic Church. Some feared that because I was a woman—a queen and not a king—I was too weak to lead the country through this

57
00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:33.078
crisis. I proved them wrong. I spoke to the troops, encouraged them to protect our shores, and assured them that they would be rewarded for doing so.

58
00:06:33.079 --> 00:06:40.364
The English navy defended our shores, then the heavens sent a storm to finish off the Armada. FAYE

59
00:06:40.365 --> 00:06:45.825
MOSS: That victory is remembered as one of the greatest accomplishments of your reign.

60
00:06:45.826 --> 00:06:51.303
Your reign also saw the beginnings of what would become a huge empire.

61
00:06:51.304 --> 00:06:58.408
QUEEN ELIZABETH I: Under my rule Sir Francis Drake became the second man to circumnavigate

62
00:06:58.409 --> 00:07:04.988
the globe. And Sir Walter Raleigh went to the New World and claimed a huge part of it for

63
00:07:04.989 --> 00:07:08.529
England. Spain had been the most powerful country in the world

64
00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:15.817
until the brave sailors of my realm challenged Spanish dominance of the seas and the New World. England

65
00:07:15.818 --> 00:07:19.903
established itself as a naval power. FAYE

66
00:07:19.904 --> 00:07:24.479
MOSS: England also saw a flowering of arts and culture.

67
00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.128
You were even a writer yourself.

68
00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:34.590
QUEEN ELIZABETH I: I translated many works throughout my life, and from time to time, I

69
00:07:34.591 --> 00:07:40.320
drafted poems. But my more important role was to inspire other writers

70
00:07:40.321 --> 00:07:51.127
and artists. We were blessed with gifted composers, painters, playwrights, and poets—including the talented Mr. Shakespeare, whose plays I adored.

71
00:07:51.128 --> 00:07:57.544
My tender care of my people gave them the conditions necessary to create art. FAYE

72
00:07:57.545 --> 00:08:01.360
MOSS: Thank you so much for being with us today, Your Majesty.

73
00:08:01.361 --> 00:08:04.820
I'll let you get back to history now.

74
00:08:04.821 --> 00:08:08.857
QUEEN ELIZABETH I:

75
00:08:08.858 --> 00:08:17.731
Thank you, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Queen Elizabeth reigned for over 44 years—one of the longest reigns of an English

76
00:08:17.732 --> 00:08:22.659
monarch at the time. She died in 1603 at the

77
00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:32.260
age of 69. Her relative King James VI of Scotland succeeded her, becoming James I of England and uniting the

78
00:08:32.261 --> 00:08:44.538
two kingdoms. The period of her reign, known as the Elizabethan Age, is still remembered for English expansion and as a golden age of English

79
00:08:44.539 --> 00:08:53.884
culture. That's all for today. Join me next week when I'll speak with another of history's superstars.

